+    // Papo start - batch 160 (R20): parked-chunk release. The batch 143 hook runs at the CARVERS
+    // exit, so chunks parked BELOW carvers (the pre-carver status ring around loaded areas,
+    // waiting on neighbour dependencies, or parked after cancelled generation) retain the whole
+    // NoiseChunk tree for as long as their holder lives: ~36KB each (interpolator double[][]
+    // slices, mapped density-function subtree, aquifer caches) - measured 318 live instances /
+    // ~11.4MB at 12 bots / vd10, and the mass scales with the pre-carver ring (player edge
+    // length, not area). A periodic main-thread sweep (ServerChunkCache) calls this for parked
+    // ProtoChunks. Identity proof for a later recreation: the only cross-step reads are
+    // preliminarySurfaceLevel (pure memoised function of the noise router, see NoiseChunk) and
+    // the aquifer (deterministic memoised noise queries); the interpolator slices are consumed
+    // exclusively inside doFill, which either already ran on the original instance or will fill
+    // a fresh one identically. Every step's getOrCreateNoiseChunk closure builds through
+    // Blender.of(region), whose result is region-radius-independent (isOldChunkAround consults
+    // the chunk map over a fixed +/-7 ring and the BlendingData collection loop is a fixed
+    // radius), and the Beardifier inputs are seed-deterministic structure data - so a chunk
+    // that resumes generation rebuilds an equivalent NoiseChunk through its own step's closure.
+    // The plain reference write cannot race a worker harmfully: an in-flight step holds its
+    // reference in a local, and a not-yet-started step simply recreates (this mirrors the
+    // batch 143 sequencing argument, extended by the read-equivalence above).
+    public boolean papoReleaseParkedNoiseChunk() {
+        if (this.noiseChunk == null) {
+            return false;
+        }
+        this.noiseChunk = null;
+        return true;
+    }
+    // Papo end - batch 160 (R20)
